<section image-layout="blank" id="cAvrtKtwPR" vertical-align="end" background-overlay="tint" background-overlay-intensity="0.42" card-margin-hidden="true"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/4D1VbII5xLvWQiUTrxfvU.png" saved-media-id="9olicug1u9r40if" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Digitally illustrated copperplate celestial engraving of a brass astronomical instrument suspended over a vast deep-space field, precise ivory celestial coordinate arcs, cyan spectral light crossing the frame and one restrained coral trace, abyssal blue ground, quiet dark space behind the lower-left title area, no explosion, no center, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="4YE3dUKzjJ" vertical-align="end"><p role="eyebrow">A navigator’s briefing</p><h1 font-size="44pt" style="padding-right: 24%;">How the Whole Sea Got Made:<br>The Big Bang, for Sailing Folk</h1><p font-size="lg" style="padding-right: 38%;">Three measurements, one history of the whole sky, and a few honest blanks on the chart.</p><spacer size="18pt"></spacer><hr color="accent" thickness="md" width="xs"><p font-size="sm">Cosmology explained by evidence, not costume</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="ofaDkTFpdr"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="D_NszqrQS4" padding="0" colspan="16"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/fCUjiO9gHNLnZhb96yIyQ.png" saved-media-id="785sz1mz73l6qvz" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Digitally illustrated copperplate celestial engraving of a navigator’s brass sighting instrument aligned with a precise star coordinate grid, cyan bearing lines crossing an abyssal-blue sky, ivory linework and restrained oxidized-brass detail, no people, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item><grid-item id="SMEiU0brng" vertical-align="center" colspan="44"><h2>You already know the method: it’s dead reckoning on the whole sky</h2><grid marker="number" marker-rule="true" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" spacing="small" style="--marker-gap: 0.6em;"><grid-item id="UDIhLwOo2y" colspan="20"><h4>Dead reckoning</h4><p>Known speed, heading and time tell you where you were six hours ago.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Z7H4vR2Q6g" colspan="20"><h4>Cosmic reckoning</h4><p>Measure how distant galaxies move apart now, then run the log backward to get a date.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="mzhsilVHuE" colspan="20"><h4>Three soundings</h4><p>Stretched starlight, light elements and a glow soaking the entire sky carry the voyage.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="6TLtN-FuTA"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="Ex9-XLqF0D"><h2>Light from distant galaxies arrives stretched</h2><p>Space grows while the light is crossing, so its waves arrive longer and shifted toward red. A farther galaxy gives the light a longer crossing and a greater stretch. Nearby systems held by gravity keep their own courses; Andromeda is coming closer.</p><custom-code>
        <style>
          .redshift-plot { height: 10.5em; padding: 0.6em 0; }
          .redshift-plot svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; }
          .redshift-plot .gridline { stroke: var(--neutral-border-color); stroke-width: 1; opacity: 0.48; }
          .redshift-plot .guide { stroke: var(--body-color); stroke-width: 1.5; stroke-dasharray: 6 8; opacity: 0.42; }
          .redshift-plot .shortwave { fill: none; stroke: var(--accent-color); stroke-width: 5; }
          .redshift-plot .longwave { fill: none; stroke: var(--palette-3); stroke-width: 5; }
          .redshift-plot .direction { fill: var(--palette-3); }
        </style>
        <div class="redshift-plot" role="img" aria-label="A short cyan light wave becomes a longer coral wave as the coordinate grid expands">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 860 170" width="100%" height="170" preserveAspectRatio="none">
            <line class="gridline" x1="15" y1="24" x2="845" y2="24"></line>
            <line class="gridline" x1="15" y1="85" x2="845" y2="85"></line>
            <line class="gridline" x1="15" y1="146" x2="845" y2="146"></line>
            <line class="guide" x1="325" y1="18" x2="390" y2="152"></line>
            <line class="guide" x1="535" y1="18" x2="470" y2="152"></line>
            <path class="shortwave" d="M25 85 C42 42 59 42 76 85 S110 128 127 85 S161 42 178 85 S212 128 229 85 S263 42 280 85 S314 128 331 85"></path>
            <polygon class="direction" points="397,72 435,85 397,98"></polygon>
            <path class="longwave" d="M475 85 C520 35 565 35 610 85 S700 135 745 85 S790 35 835 85"></path>
          </svg>
        </div>
      </custom-code><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="gm9e3ASu5G" colspan="20"><h4>Leaves</h4><p>Shorter wavelength.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="PrsbQsUPuT" colspan="20"><h4>Crossing</h4><p>Space grows in flight.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="4vMw3aFYu3" colspan="20"><h4>Arrives</h4><p>Longer wavelength: redshift.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption">First sounding · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-behind-the-discoveries/hubble-big-bang/">NASA, Hubble science</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="xbdDa1Ddl6"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="inlWTVe_l2"><h2>It was not a blast, and it has no center</h2><p>Nothing exploded into empty space. Distances between distant galaxies grow everywhere at once, and every observer sees the same spreading in every direction. There is no port you could sail to and call the origin. It happened everywhere, including here.</p><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="LO2aizAu8D" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4>Earlier</h4><custom-code>
            <style>
              .early-field { height: 9em; }
              .early-field svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; }
              .early-field .link { stroke: var(--neutral-border-color); stroke-width: 2; stroke-dasharray: 5 7; }
              .early-field .point { fill: var(--accent-color); }
              .early-field .signal { fill: var(--palette-3); }
            </style>
            <div class="early-field" role="img" aria-label="A compact pattern of galaxies connected by short separations">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 360 150" width="100%" height="150">
                <line class="link" x1="88" y1="95" x2="160" y2="42"></line>
                <line class="link" x1="160" y1="42" x2="258" y2="82"></line>
                <line class="link" x1="258" y1="82" x2="205" y2="126"></line>
                <line class="link" x1="205" y1="126" x2="88" y2="95"></line>
                <line class="link" x1="160" y1="42" x2="205" y2="126"></line>
                <circle class="point" cx="88" cy="95" r="8"></circle>
                <circle class="point" cx="160" cy="42" r="8"></circle>
                <circle class="signal" cx="258" cy="82" r="8"></circle>
                <circle class="point" cx="205" cy="126" r="8"></circle>
              </svg>
            </div>
          </custom-code><p>One pattern, with smaller separations.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="UoISw7XniT" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h4>Later</h4><custom-code>
            <style>
              .later-field { height: 9em; }
              .later-field svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; }
              .later-field .link { stroke: var(--neutral-border-color); stroke-width: 2; stroke-dasharray: 5 7; }
              .later-field .point { fill: var(--accent-color); }
              .later-field .signal { fill: var(--palette-3); }
            </style>
            <div class="later-field" role="img" aria-label="The same pattern of galaxies with every separation enlarged">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 360 150" width="100%" height="150">
                <line class="link" x1="38" y1="104" x2="143" y2="18"></line>
                <line class="link" x1="143" y1="18" x2="325" y2="74"></line>
                <line class="link" x1="325" y1="74" x2="224" y2="142"></line>
                <line class="link" x1="224" y1="142" x2="38" y2="104"></line>
                <line class="link" x1="143" y1="18" x2="224" y2="142"></line>
                <circle class="point" cx="38" cy="104" r="8"></circle>
                <circle class="point" cx="143" cy="18" r="8"></circle>
                <circle class="signal" cx="325" cy="74" r="8"></circle>
                <circle class="point" cx="224" cy="142" r="8"></circle>
              </svg>
            </div>
          </custom-code><p>The same pattern, with larger separations. No origin is marked.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption">Schematic, not to scale · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-behind-the-discoveries/hubble-big-bang/">NASA, Hubble science</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="L81G80txFB"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="FY4fvf1b3c" background-overlay="tint" background-overlay-intensity="0.58" vertical-align="center" padding="30pt" colspan="22"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/bJHTxhp1-g6vdkdjRLL_c.png" saved-media-id="qjr3o18ck6uu29u" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Digitally illustrated copperplate celestial engraving of deep cosmic layers receding through a precise brass coordinate instrument, ivory arcs tightening toward a dark ancient horizon, cyan spectral traces and restrained coral markers on abyssal blue, no explosion, no central point, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"><display size="xl">13.8</display><h3>billion years</h3><p>The age found by running the sky’s log backward.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="MbFJryAj-w" vertical-align="center" colspan="38"><h2>Run the log back and you get about 13.8 billion years</h2><p>If distances are growing now, matter was packed tighter before, and the universe grows hotter the further back you reckon.</p><grid marker="number" marker-rule="true" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" vertical-sizing="hug" style="--marker-gap: 0.6em;"><grid-item id="SZeMuhBorL" colspan="15"><h4>Now</h4><p>Distant galaxies are separating.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="uSdF1atp_x" colspan="15"><h4>Earlier</h4><p>Their distances were smaller.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Gg7u3sHMCm" colspan="15"><h4>Further back</h4><p>Matter was tighter and hotter.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="7JPSxBps9k" colspan="15"><h4>The fitted age</h4><p>About 13.8 billion years.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption"><span text-color="palette-4">Note. The Planck mission’s fit: 13.797 billion years, give or take 23 million. <a href="https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2020/09/aa33910-18/T1.html">Planck results</a></span></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="7fdhT4W6ge" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="49s5wU_oAh"><h2>The first minutes forged helium, not hydrogen</h2><grid marker="number" marker-size="large" marker-style="subtle" marker-shape="chevron" marker-position="above" marker-align="center" marker-fill="true" spacing="none" padding="1em" style="--marker-chip-padding: 0.75em;"><grid-item id="jm2zUiRZav" colspan="15"><h4>One second</h4><p>A soup of light and particles sat near ten billion degrees Celsius.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="0wUNo4W5lE" colspan="15"><h4>Primordial protons</h4><p>Most ordinary hydrogen nuclei are single protons that never fused.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="a3f9hYcaEl" colspan="15"><h4>Next few minutes</h4><p>Neutrons joined protons into deuterium; deuterium built helium.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="OFxBo1Gtpz" colspan="15"><h4>About five minutes</h4><p>Cooling shut the forge. Measured deuterium and helium match the prediction.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption">Second sounding · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/universe/overview/">NASA universe overview</a> · <a href="https://pdg.lbl.gov/2025/reviews/rpp2025-rev-bbang-nucleosynthesis.pdf">Particle Data Group</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="PDYAXHaLFy"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="GXrHfsdm5i"><h2>380,000 years on, the fog lifted and light ran free</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="QoECFPfRGr" background-overlay="tint" background-overlay-intensity="0.58" vertical-align="end" colspan="30"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/FzbrJj169TiRl5l2_pNQN.png" saved-media-id="dvsaczaqwgm149x" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Digitally illustrated copperplate celestial engraving of a diffuse centerless plasma fog filling deep space, ivory photon paths scattering repeatedly among luminous particles, cyan glints diffused through abyssal blue and restrained oxidized-brass detail, no explosion, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><p role="eyebrow">Before</p><h3>A glowing fog</h3><p>Loose electrons scattered light constantly. Nuclei could not yet hold them.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="8R6VE7TCqJ" background-overlay="tint" background-overlay-intensity="0.48" vertical-align="end" colspan="30"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/rXlamX3eCD1akmpIEgyse.png" saved-media-id="qh6qwfba3ev0hgf" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Digitally illustrated copperplate celestial engraving of a transparent centerless cosmic field after neutral atoms form, one broad relic-light horizon travelling freely through abyssal blue, crisp ivory linework, cyan spectral glow and restrained coral accents, no explosion, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><p role="eyebrow">After</p><h3>Neutral atoms formed</h3><p>Nuclei captured electrons. Light stopped scattering and has travelled freely since.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption">This is the oldest light an instrument can see; earlier than it, the record is fog. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-behind-the-discoveries/hubble-big-bang/">NASA</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="2ITsRafZOT"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="muivLPLW9I" vertical-align="center" colspan="36"><labels><label variant="solid">Holmdel, New Jersey · 1965</label></labels><h2>Two engineers found that light in 1965 while chasing a hiss</h2><p>Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson measured about 3.5 K more noise than expected with a horn antenna.</p><grid divider="sm neutral-border" direction="vertical" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="Dszfk32-5v" padding="9pt 0"><h4>They checked the instrument</h4><p>Equipment and surroundings did not explain it.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="pBeHP7zK3p" padding="9pt 0"><h4>The hiss stayed</h4><p>It was the same in every direction, unpolarized and unchanged through the seasons.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="LOAdcOQK0y" padding="9pt 0"><h4>The sky supplied the answer</h4><p>Relic light, cooled to a few degrees above absolute zero.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption">Third sounding · <a href="https://doi.org/10.1086/148307">Penzias and Wilson, 1965</a></p></grid-item><grid-item id="puplcSFLja" padding="0" colspan="24"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/DbkRAEsAR7YLSg9xh-awN.png" saved-media-id="chquxql2xb0jhl7" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Digitally illustrated copperplate scientific engraving of the large horn antenna at Holmdel, New Jersey, seen from a low three-quarter angle under a deep nocturnal sky, precise ivory structural lines, oxidized-brass instrument details and a faint cyan all-sky signal, no people, no labels or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="0gE4-QKBts" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="SrXPyxa1a3" vertical-align="center" colspan="23"><h2>Three soundings agree, which is why the model holds</h2><p>Each was measured independently. A rival account has to explain all three at once.</p><p role="caption"><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-behind-the-discoveries/hubble-big-bang/">Stretched light</a> · <a href="https://pdg.lbl.gov/2025/reviews/rpp2025-rev-bbang-nucleosynthesis.pdf">Light elements</a> · <a href="https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2020/09/aa33910-18/T1.html">Whole-sky glow</a></p></grid-item><grid-item id="X13Gc6jpXk" align="center" vertical-align="center" colspan="37"><diagram template-key="inputs" align="center" width="520pt" height="340pt" data-meta="{&quot;general&quot;:[{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>One expanding history</h4>&quot;}],&quot;step&quot;:[{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Stretched light</h4><p>Distant galaxies</p>&quot;},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Light elements</h4><p>Deuterium and helium</p>&quot;},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Whole-sky glow</h4><p>Relic light and its ripples</p>&quot;}]}"></diagram></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="Rqm7rx9Ksk" background-overlay="tint" background-overlay-intensity="0.48" card-margin-hidden="true"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/T3Zc1r0xWaqqsioQ4TyRh.png" saved-media-id="dlba1ozv84aazlr" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Digitally illustrated copperplate celestial chart fading into a broad unsounded dark region at the left edge, a faint relic-light horizon crossing the abyssal-blue field from every direction, precise ivory coordinate traces, restrained oxidized-brass details, cyan glow and one coral anomaly marker, no physical edge, no explosion, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><freeform><box type="content" x="56" y="46" w="790"><p role="eyebrow">Limits of the model</p><h1 font-size="42pt">Where the chart still runs blank</h1><p font-size="lg">A sound model marks the reach of its evidence, then stops.</p></box><box type="content" x="56" y="230" w="396" h="220" sizing="fixed" background-color="rgba(7,20,33,0.88)" style="padding: 18pt 20pt; border: 1pt solid var(--neutral-border-color); border-radius: 4pt;"><labels><label variant="outline">Boundary</label></labels><h3 font-size="20pt">The earliest moment is unsounded</h3><p font-size="15pt">The model runs from a fraction of a second onward. What set it off, and whether “before” is even a sensible word, lies beyond the evidence.</p><p role="caption"><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/universe/overview/">NASA universe overview</a></p></box><box type="content" x="476" y="230" w="428" h="220" sizing="fixed" background-color="rgba(7,20,33,0.88)" style="padding: 18pt 20pt; border: 1pt solid var(--neutral-border-color); border-radius: 4pt;"><labels><label variant="outline">Open sounding</label></labels><h3 font-size="20pt">Lithium does not match</h3><p font-size="15pt">The forge predicts roughly three times more lithium than astronomers find in the oldest stars. Nobody has settled why.</p><p role="caption"><a href="https://pdg.lbl.gov/2025/reviews/rpp2025-rev-bbang-nucleosynthesis.pdf">Particle Data Group</a></p></box><box type="content" x="56" y="478" w="780"><p>The ancient glow keeps arriving from every direction while you stand on deck.</p></box></freeform></section>